library(fixest); library(haven); library(car); library(dplyr)
load(url("https://raw.githubusercontent.com/anzonyquispe/did_book/main/cc_xd_didtextbook_2025_9_30/Data%20sets/Moser%20and%20Voena%202012/moser_voena_didtextbook.RData"))
formula_str <- paste("patents ~",
paste(paste0("reltimeminus", 1:18), collapse = " + "), "+",
paste(paste0("reltimeplus", 1:21), collapse = " + "),
"| year^patents1900 + treatmentgroup")
model_controls <- feols(as.formula(formula_str), data = df, cluster = ~subclass)
print(coeftable(model_controls), digits = 6)
ftest <- linearHypothesis(model_controls, paste0("reltimeminus", 1:18))
cat("\nChisq =", round(ftest$Chisq[2], 6), " p-value =", format(ftest$`Pr(>Chisq)`[2], scientific = TRUE), "\n")
This gives the same results, since with `reset(#)` standard errors are clustered at the original group level anyway. The copy must have a different name: `cluster(G)` is ignored by the command, and the error comes back.
---
title: "issue"
## Bug: `by()` + `reset(#)` returns `old_group_XX not found`
### Problem
When `by()` and `reset(#)` are combined (and no `cluster()` is specified), the command runs for the first level of the `by()` variable but fails for the second one with `old_group_XX not found`.
